[# Section 12 — Frequently Asked Questions. Back-matter; appears
   after §11 glossary. Authored 2026-05-22.

   These are the questions a first-week CX developer actually asks.
   Each Q has a one-paragraph recovery pattern with a concrete
   command or code snippet. Where the answer is normative, the FAQ
   defers to the spec via a markdown link. Cross-links to other
   sections use [[anchor]] form. File extension .cxd — data-only
   source. #]

[section id=faq group='Reference' n=12 title='Frequently Asked Questions'

  [intro
    """If you ran into something confusing in your first week of CX,
       it is probably here. Each answer leads with the recovery
       command and follows up with the why. Grouped by topic; use
       your editor's search if you arrived from a search engine."""]

  [child id=faq-setup n=12.1 title='Setup and install'

    [child id=faq-not-found title='Why does `cx --version` fail with "command not found"?'
      [body
        """`cx` is on disk but not on your shell `PATH`. The installer
           (`curl -sSL https://cxhome.org/install | sh`) puts the
           binary under its `PREFIX`, `~/.local` unless you set one, so
           it lives at `~/.local/bin/cx`: confirm it with
           `ls -l ~/.local/bin/cx`, add the directory for this shell
           with `export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"`, and put the same
           line in your shell's startup file to keep it. A source build
           leaves the binary where the build wrote it — see
           [[install-status]]. Then check that `cx` answers: a
           one-line program with `-e`, which prints its value:"""]
      [fixture id=cli-e-one-liner]]

    [child id=faq-v-not-found title='Build fails with "V not found"'
      [body
        """The reference build uses devbox to pin the V compiler
           version. If you ran `make build` outside a devbox shell
           and got "V not found," either install V manually
           (https://vlang.io) or — better — wrap the build in
           devbox so the toolchain matches CI:
           `devbox run -- make build`."""]
      [note
        """Devbox is at https://www.jetify.com/devbox — `brew
           install jetify-com/devbox/devbox` or
           `curl -fsSL https://get.jetify.com/devbox | bash`."""]]

    [child id=faq-windows title='Can I use CX on Windows?'
      [body
        """Today: yes, via WSL2 (Ubuntu or Debian inside Windows).
           The Linux build runs unchanged. Native Windows — a
           PE-format `cx.exe` and `cxlib.dll` — is on the
           roadmap; the bottleneck is the V toolchain's Windows
           support, not CX itself. Track issue
           [#windows-native](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/issues/windows-native)
           for the current state."""]]]

  [child id=faq-parsing n=12.2 title='Parsing and syntax'

    [child id=faq-find-retired title='Why does my `[?find]` fail with CXER0100?'
      [body
        """`[?find]` was retired in v0.8.0 — it was [; version-literal-ok ]
           always sugar for `[?for]` over a pattern. Rewrite each
           `[?find pattern [yield body]]` to either a direct CXPath
           (when the pattern is just an element with optional
           predicates) or to `[?for]` (when the pattern is
           structurally more complex). See [[from-v07x]] for the
           full mapping table."""]]

    [child id=faq-attr-vs-slot title='Why does `[user :id 42]` parse differently from `[user id=42]`?'
      [body
        """They are two different syntactic forms that both parse.
           `name=value` — the data-element *attribute* syntax, per
           `spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf [55] Attribute` —
           attaches a named value to the element.
           `:name` on its own is an **atom literal** — so
           `[user :id 42]` is a `user` element whose body is two
           items: the atom `:id` and the int `42`. No error is
           raised; it just doesn't mean "the id attribute is 42".
           Inside a `[?directive …]`, the same `:name value` spelling
           is the *labeled-slot* syntax (per
           `spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf [127e]`), so
           `:id 42` there is the slot named `id` bound to `42`. And
           if you wanted a type annotation, that is the double-colon
           glue: `[user::int 42]`. The JSON projection tells the two
           data forms apart — the attribute becomes a key, the atom
           body a list of its two items:"""]
      [fixture id=atom-028-attribute-vs-atom-body-json]
      [note
        """The two-form distinction is by design — see the body
           discussion at [[attributes]]."""]]

    [child id=faq-count-container title='What is the difference between `[…]` and `(…)` collections?'
      [body
        """A sequence (`(…)`) is the flat iteration shape — every CX
           value is a sequence, directives iterate them, and a sequence
           never nests inside another. An array (`[…]`) is a container
           Item that **preserves nesting**: a matrix written as an array
           of three rows is ONE item of its element, and the rows stay
           rows — not nine numbers:"""]
      [fixture id=program-cxpath-g2-10-array-literal]
      [body
        """The distinction is the **container vs atom** principle in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md) §2.0. See
           [[container-atom]] for the full rules; sequences are flat,
           arrays are containers."""]]

    [child id=faq-hash-mismatch title='Why does my hash differ on Linux vs macOS?'
      [body
        """It should not. `cx hash` is the SHA-256 of the strict
           canonical bytes, and those are platform-independent by
           construction: the canonical form drops comments, normalises
           quotes and whitespace, and always writes LF line endings. The
           same document written two ways is one digest:"""]
      [fixture id=cli-hash-presentation-a]
      [fixture id=cli-hash-presentation-b]
      [body
        """What the canonical form never normalises is attribute order —
           it is data in CX
           ([`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md)
           §2.1), and strings are stored as written, with no Unicode
           normalisation — so two attributes swapped are a different
           document and a different digest:"""]
      [fixture id=cli-hash-attribute-order]
      [body
        """Run `cx hash` on both platforms over the same bytes. If the
           digests differ, that is a defect and a release blocker."""]
      [note
        """File a bug at https://github.com/cx-home/cx/issues with
           the source document and the divergent hashes from both
           platforms. Include `cx --version` output from each."""]]

    [child id=faq-attr-reorder title='Does the JSON projection reorder my attributes?'
      [body
        """No — attribute order is data in CX, and nothing sorts it.
           The canonical form keeps the authored order (canonical.md
           §2.1: attribute order is never a normalisation axis), so
           `cx fmt` tidies the spacing and the quotes and leaves the
           order alone, and the JSON projection writes its keys in the
           same order:"""]
      [fixture id=cli-fmt-keeps-attribute-order]
      [fixture id=cli-json-keeps-attribute-order]
      [body
        """Because the order is part of the document, two orders are
           two identities — the hash question above shows the two
           digests."""]]

    [child id=faq-double-anchor title='Can I have two `&anchor` declarations with the same name?'
      [body
        """You can, and it is not an error: when a name is declared twice
           the first definition in document order wins, deterministically
           ([`spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md)
           §2.8, rule 6). Here the merge `[c *x]` and the alias `[*x]`
           both resolve to the first `&x`, and the strict form strips
           both declarations:"""]
      [fixture id=id-037-duplicate-anchor-first-definition-wins]
      [body
        """So keep a name to one declaration, and to reuse its content,
           declare it once and merge it where you need it with `*name` —
           a host attribute of the same name replaces the merged one in
           its place:"""]
      [fixture id=id-035-merge-inlines-with-host-override]]]

  [child id=faq-code n=12.3 title='Code language'

    [child id=faq-find-vs-for title='What is the difference between `[?for]` and the old `[?find]`?'
      [body
        """They are the same operation. `[?find]` was retired because it was always
           pattern-driven `[?for]` with one less keyword. Anything
           you wrote in `[?find]` form can be expressed as `[?for]`
           over a pattern, or — more directly — as a CXPath
           expression. See [[from-v07x]] for the migration table. The
           pattern form, as it reads today:"""]
      [fixture id=program-for-006-conditional-emit]]

    [child id=faq-dispatch-on-type title='How do I dispatch on element type?'
      [body
        """Use multi-arm `[?match]` with `[case …]` clause-children
           that name the element type. Each `[case …]` is a pattern +
           result, the first one that matches wins, and `[else …]` is
           the catch-all. Here a `[prose]` node takes the first arm:"""]
      [fixture id=program-match-multi-001-element-dispatch]
      [body
        """Put the dispatch inside a `[?for]` over `//doc/*` and every
           child takes the arm its element name picks."""]]

    [child id=faq-update-value title='How do I update a value?'
      [body
        """Use `[?modify]`. It takes a document, a CXPath
           focus, and an action; it returns a new document. There is
           no in-place mutation in CX — see the next question."""]
      [fixture id=program-modify-001-set-attribute]
      [body
        """Eleven actions, one per clause: `[set …]`, `[delete]`,
           `[using …]`, `[rename …]`, `[set-attr …]`, `[delete-attr …]`,
           `[append …]`, `[prepend …]`, `[insert-before …]`,
           `[insert-after …]` and `[replace …]`."""]]

    [child id=faq-pure-functional title='Why does `[?modify]` return a new Doc instead of mutating?'
      [body
        """Pure-functional semantics — no in-place writes in CX.
           Concurrency is safe by construction (no reader ever
           observes a half-modified tree), equality is trivially
           preservable (the old hash still names the old document),
           and structural sharing makes it cheap: unchanged branches
           of the tree are reused between the input and the output,
           so `[?modify]` is O(log n) in the document size for a
           single-focus update. See [[pure-updates]]."""]]

    [child id=faq-pipeline-implicit-bind title='What does `_` mean in a pipeline stage?'
      [body
        """`_` is the partial-application hole: a `[?pipe]` stage
           that carries a single `_` receives the threaded value at
           that position. A stage with no hole receives the value
           appended as its final positional argument — a bare name like
           `add1` is called with it. The hole form first, then the bare name:"""]
      [fixture id=program-ophole-007-pipe-stage-one-hole]
      [fixture id=program-sap-pipe-01-bare-stages]]]

  [child id=faq-bindings n=12.4 title='Bindings'

    [child id=faq-which-binding title='Which binding should I use?'
      [body
        """Pick by deployment shape. The **native reference binding**
           for embedded work — no FFI overhead, the smallest binary
           footprint. The **scripting binding** for data-engineering
           pipelines and analytical work already living in a dataframe
           library. The **services binding** for high-throughput
           servers, especially when CX is the wire format. The
           **systems binding** for the lowest-overhead FFI into a
           systems language's crates. Layer 1 (the 16 canonical
           methods) is identical across all four — [[bindings]] names
           each one beside its repository and its ABI vector — so you
           can change your mind later."""]]

    [child id=faq-thread-safe title='Are CX values thread-safe?'
      [body
        """Yes. CX values are immutable by construction — every
           update returns a new value via structural sharing.
           Multiple threads can read the same `Doc` concurrently
           without locks. Bindings handle their own GC threading
           via `cx_thread_register` (see next)."""]]

    [child id=faq-cx-thread-register title='When do I call `cx_thread_register()`?'
      [body
        """It depends on the binding. **The systems binding**: never
           — every FFI chokepoint calls `ensure_thread` before the C
           call and unregisters at thread exit. **The services
           binding**: never — each foreign call runs on a locked OS
           thread that the internal guard registers. **The scripting
           binding**: it does NOT register threads at all; it assumes
           the interpreter's global lock plus a single calling thread,
           so a host thread that calls into libcx is unprotected —
           keep libcx calls on one thread, or call
           `cx_thread_register` yourself before the first call from
           a worker thread. **Manually always** when you call the C
           ABI directly from a host-spawned thread — that thread is
           opaque to libcx's Boehm GC until it registers. See
           [[ffi-thread-register]] and
           [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §1.6."""]]

    [child id=faq-doc-memory title='Do I need to close a Doc to release memory?'
      [body
        """No — there is nothing to close. In the scripting binding a
           `Doc` is an ordinary refcounted host object: the binding
           copies every string libcx returns and frees the C buffer at
           the call boundary, so no Doc holds libcx memory between
           calls. Dropping the last reference reclaims it like any
           other host value. `Doc` has no `close()`, no finalizer, and
           no scoped-resource protocol."""]
      [body
        """The handle-backed classes are the ones with an explicit
           lifecycle — `Stream`, `TableReader`, `TableWriter`, and
           `EventWriter` carry `close()` and support the host's
           scoped-resource form, so a reader opened over a columnar
           file is closed on the way out of the block whatever the
           block did; the shape, per binding, is on [[bindings]]."""]
      [note
        """See [[ffi-doc-lifetime]] for the full per-binding lifetime
           contract. An archived binding's collector interaction notes
           live in that binding's own repository, frozen with it."""]]]

  [child id=faq-tooling n=12.5 title='Tooling'

    [child id=faq-lint-tree title='How do I lint a tree of CX files?'
      [body
        """`cx lint` works file by file; for a tree, hand it every file
           `find` lists: `find . -name '*.cx' -print0 | xargs -0 cx lint`.
           One file and its finding — an anchor nothing references:"""]
      [fixture id=cli-lint-unused-anchor]
      [body
        """`cx lint` runs the built-in rule set (CX-L001 and
           CX-L003 through CX-L008; the codes are registered in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
           §9.4/§9.5). Exit codes are the CLI convention: 0 = no
           findings at or above `--fail-on`, 1 = findings, 2 = I/O
           or parse failure. Per-project configuration lives in a
           `.cxlint.cx` file, discovered by walking up from the
           linted file. See [[lint]] for the rule list."""]]

    [child id=faq-semantic-diff title='How do I diff two CX files semantically?'
      [body
        """`cx diff a.cx b.cx` — the diff is always semantic:
           it walks the strict-canonical forms of both inputs, so
           attribute order, quote choice, and comment-only changes
           produce no delta. There is no `--semantic` flag because
           there is no other mode. Output shape is
           `--format=unified|json|summary` (default unified), with
           `--color[=always|never|auto]` / `--no-color` for the
           unified view. Here the second file re-quotes, re-spaces and
           comments the first and changes one value; the diff names the
           value alone and exits 1, because there is a change:"""]
      [fixture id=cli-diff-semantic]
      [body
        """See [[diff]] for the algorithm and the JSON-output mode
           used by CI integrations."""]]

    [child id=faq-make-docs title='How do I run the canonical guide locally?'
      [body
        """The guide is generated by the top-level `Makefile`. From a
           devbox shell, `devbox run -- make guide` renders it into
           `docs/guide/`, and `devbox run -- make guide-http` renders it
           and serves it locally, with the headers the playground's
           threaded build needs. `make docs` regenerates the LLM layer
           under `docs/llm/` and runs every documentation check."""]
      [body
        """The generator reads `docs-src/canonical/manifest.cxd`,
           assembles the section files, replays every `[fixture]` a
           page cites against the running `cx` — a case whose output
           moved stops the render, naming it — and writes the rendered
           pages. See [[docgen]]."""]]

    [child id=faq-vscode-highlight title="Why doesn't VS Code highlight my CX file?"
      [body
        """The CX VS Code extension is not auto-installed, and no
           prebuilt `.vsix` is committed — build one first with
           `npm run package` in `tooling/vscode/`, then from the
           command palette run "Extensions: Install from VSIX…"
           and point at the produced file. After
           install, run "Developer: Reload Window" to pick up the
           grammar. The extension contributes the `.cx`, `.cxs`,
           and `.cxd` file associations; if a file is mis-detected,
           click the language label in the status bar and switch
           manually."""]
      [note
        """If you are on Neovim, the install script is
           `tooling/install/install-nvim.sh` — it wires
           tree-sitter and the LSP. See
           [[editors]] for Helix and other editors."""]]]

  [child id=faq-perf n=12.6 title='Performance'

    [child id=faq-max-file-size title='How big a file can cx handle?'
      [body
        """Parsing is the default action — `cx FILE` parses and
           evaluates — and there is no configured size cap: the
           whole-document path is memory-bound, so the practical
           limit is the RAM the parsed tree needs. For inputs too
           big to materialize, use the streaming surfaces — the
           events pull API at the ABI (`cx_events_open` /
           `cx_events_next`) and streamed `[?for]` comprehension
           output — which bound resident memory to a chunk. See
           [[streaming-data]]. The release gate holds streaming
           throughput to a ≥200 MB/s floor; that gate does not pass
           yet (`[?for]` measures around the line, `[?map]` below
           it), so treat the floor as the target, not a promise.
           `cx big.cx` reads the whole document, which is fine while the
           tree fits in memory; the streamed shape is a comprehension
           over the document bound with `--data`:"""]
      [fixture id=program-for-002-active-emails]]

    [child id=faq-single-threaded title='Is parsing single-threaded?'
      [body
        """The **parser** is single-threaded per document — the
           bracket grammar is inherently sequential. The
           **streaming evaluator** is single-threaded per stream
           but multiple streams can run concurrently, and a `[par]`
           clause on `[?map]` / `[?reduce]` parallelises across
           bounded worker pools. For analytical workloads on
           tabular data, CXCol scans are SIMD-accelerated where
           the host CPU supports it."""]]

    [child id=faq-playground-slower title='Why is the playground slower than running cx in the terminal?'
      [body
        """The playground is a constrained demonstration surface
           bundled into the browser. It carries unavoidable
           overhead — Asyncify instrumentation (so `[?sleep]` can
           yield through the JS event loop), pthreads runtime (so
           `[par]` runs real OS threads), and the wasm format
           itself. Production CX deployments use the native CLI
           (a ~10 MB binary), the language bindings, or libcx as a
           shared library (~12 MB) directly —
           those paths have no Asyncify overhead, no
           SharedArrayBuffer requirement, no bundle-size
           pressure. The playground exists to make CX explorable
           from any browser without an install step; native CX
           is the performance target. See [[wasm-story]] for the
           full framing, including the three playground delivery
           modes (GitHub Pages, file://, `make guide-http`)."""]]

    [child id=faq-playground-par-no-speedup title='I ran [?map [par]] in the playground and it did not speed up — bug?'
      [body
        """Not a bug — your playground is most likely running in
           single-thread mode. Three delivery modes: GitHub Pages
           and `file://` both serve the
           single-threaded Asyncify build (no cross-origin
           isolation possible without custom HTTP headers, and
           browsers gate SharedArrayBuffer behind that). `[par]`
           is honoured (output multiset is correct, sequential is
           one valid ordering) but doesn't accelerate. For real
           parallel speedup in the playground, run
           `make guide-http` — the V veb server sends the
           required cross-origin-isolation headers, the
           pthreads-enabled wasm loads, and `[par]` uses real OS
           threads. The playground's adaptive footer tells you
           which mode you're in."""]]

    [child id=faq-parquet-perf title='How fast is Parquet round-trip vs native CXCol?'
      [body
        """There is no maintained benchmark number to quote for the
           Parquet path. Structurally, Parquet writes pay for
           compression and the Arrow → Parquet conversion; native
           **CXCol** skips compression by default and uses a wire
           format designed for the CX value model, so it is the
           cheaper CX-internal path. Rule of thumb: use
           **Parquet** for interchange with the broader analytics
           ecosystem, **CXCol** for CX-internal pipelines. See
           [[cxcol-vs-others]] for the full decision matrix."""]]]

  [child id=faq-security n=12.7 title='Security'

    [child id=faq-include-untrusted title='Is `[?cx include]` safe with untrusted input?'
      [body
        """Yes by default — because by default nothing is granted.
           CX is deny-by-default: with no `--allow-*` flags the
           evaluation is pure-only, so includes (which ride the
           `read` capability) stay unresolved and no directive can
           touch the filesystem or network. That makes the bare
           invocation the safe one for untrusted input — an include
           reaching outside its directory is left exactly as written:"""]
      [fixture id=cli-include-ungranted-unresolved]
      [body
        """Trusted input that needs its includes grants the read and
           names the root they resolve against:
           `cx --allow-read --include-root=./conf main.cx`.
           The full capability model lives at
           [[include-capabilities]] and in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/security.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/security.md).
           The `cx_features()` bitmask exposes what the loaded libcx
           supports; the grant set gates what the runtime
           permits."""]]

    [child id=faq-resource-limits title='How do I limit resource use?'
      [body
        """CX has no environment-variable cap family — there is no
           parse-size, depth, or node-count knob. What bounds a run:
           the **capability grant set** (deny-by-default `--allow-*`)
           gates every effect an evaluation can perform, the
           **streaming surfaces** bound resident memory to a chunk,
           and per-process ceilings come from the operating system
           (`ulimit -v 1048576` before `cx input.cx` caps the address
           space at 1 GiB; cgroups and container limits work the same)
           like any other process. See [[limits]] and [[security]]. An
           effect the grant set does not hold is refused, as a value:"""]
      [fixture id=io-001-read-file-cap-denied]]]

  [child id=faq-migration n=12.8 title='Migration'

    [child id=faq-migrate-v07 title='How do I migrate from v0.7.x?' [; version-literal-ok ]
      [body
        """v0.8.0 is the pre-release API/format-stability boundary. [; version-literal-ok ]
           The changes are mechanical
           renames plus the `[?find]` → `[?for]`/CXPath rewrite.
           The full diff between v0.7.x and v0.8.0 is documented at [; version-literal-ok ]
           [[from-v07x]]; the non-mechanical cases (custom directive
           names that collide with new core directives) are listed
           at the bottom of that section. If you do need to migrate
           a v0.7.x codebase, the rename tables there give [; version-literal-ok ]
           you everything to do it by hand or with a one-off
           project-local script."""]]

    [child id=faq-archived-bindings title='What if I am on TypeScript / Java / C# / Ruby / Kotlin / Swift binding?'
      [body
        """Those bindings were archived in v0.8.0 — they are still [; version-literal-ok ]
           available on v0.7.5 (last release before the archive) [; version-literal-ok ]
           and each keeps its own repository, frozen at the ABI it
           was archived with (the `ships=archived` rows of the
           repository map). The current binding set is the four
           shipped binding repositories [[bindings]] names. The path
           forward is to **migrate to a shipped binding** — Layer 1 is
           identical across all four, so the migration is mostly
           module-import changes plus host-idiomatic adjustments at
           Layer 2. (There is no supported pre-reshape line to stay on
           — no maintenance releases are cut for tags before the
           stability boundary.) Each archived repository's README
           carries its archival notes and the pointer to the last
           working commit."""]
      [note
        """The decision to archive was about resource focus, not
           technical limitation — those bindings worked. The shipped
           set was chosen for ecosystem reach (a scripting binding for
           analytics, a services binding, a systems binding, and the
           native reference). Community-maintained revivals are
           welcome and the C ABI stays stable for them."""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/faq.cx during Phase B docs →
     guide consolidation (2026-05-24). Unique Q&A from the
     pre-Phase-B docs FAQ that complement §12.1-§12.8 above. #]

  [child id=faq-first-thirty n=12.9 title='First thirty minutes — quick answers'
    [intro
      """Working answers to questions adopters ask in the first
         thirty minutes. The §12.1-§12.8 children above cover
         deeper operational topics; this section is the
         beginner-question backstop."""]

    [child id=faq-leading-zero n=12.9.1 title='Why does [zip 02134] produce a string?'
      [body
        """Leading-zero tokens stay strings unless the prefix is
           `0x` (hex), `0o` (octal), or `0b` (binary). The rule
           is deliberately predictable: leading-zero numerics are
           typically identifiers (postal codes, area codes, BIC
           codes, account numbers) where the leading zero is
           data, not a numeric value. CX's design directly
           inverts YAML's "country: NO" footgun."""]]

    [child id=faq-decimal n=12.9.2 title='What is :decimal for?'
      [body
        """Arbitrary-precision decimal — for money, scientific
           quantities, or any value where IEEE 754 binary floats
           would lose precision. `0.1 + 0.2` is
           `0.30000000000000004` in float; in `:decimal` it is
           `0.3`. A binding maps `:decimal` to its host's
           arbitrary-precision decimal type, never to a binary
           float."""]]

    [child id=faq-brackets n=12.9.3 title='Why brackets instead of braces, indentation, or tags?'
      [body
        """Brackets are the lightest balanced delimiter not in
           heavy use already. Braces are JSON; mixing braces with
           the rest of CX would create constant visual collision.
           Indentation breaks under programmatic generation.
           Tags double the character count for every construct
           and force closing-tag repetition. Brackets also pun
           usefully: `[name]` is the thing called `name`, which
           matches how humans write structured outlines on
           whiteboards."""]]

    [child id=faq-logfmt n=12.9.4 title='What is logfmt mode?'
      [body
        """A CX file made entirely of bare top-level `key=value`
           attributes. Each line reads as one anonymous `_` element
           carrying that line's attributes, auto-typed like any other
           attribute, so a log stream is a CX document — it projects,
           queries and hashes like one, with no separate logfmt
           parser:"""]
      [fixture id=logfmt-001-each-line-one-element]]

    [child id=faq-thread-safety n=12.9.5 title='Is CX thread-safe?'
      [body
        """The C ABI is documented per-symbol with thread-safety
           classes. All format converters are stateless and can
           run concurrently across threads on disjoint inputs.
           The Boehm GC underneath libcx requires
           `cx_thread_register` on each calling thread. The systems
           and services bindings handle that automatically
           (per-chokepoint registration; locked OS threads); the
           scripting binding does NOT — it assumes the interpreter's
           global lock and a single calling thread, so keep its libcx
           calls on one thread."""]]

    [child id=faq-confusion-raw n=12.9.6 title='Why does my embedded code get garbled?'
      [body
        """The bracket-pipe block-content form keeps its bytes but still
           reads a `[` as CX, so an index like `arr[i]` inside it opens
           an element named `i`. Use the bracket-hash raw-text form
           instead — nothing inside is parsed, and the terminator is
           `#]`. The brackets, the `<` and the braces come back byte
           for byte:"""]
      [fixture id=012-rawtext]]

    [child id=faq-license n=12.9.7 title='License and contributing'
      [body
        """**License:** Apache 2.0. **Contributing:** the project
           lives at github.com/cx-home/cx — bug reports, doc
           fixes, and pull requests welcome. For format-design
           questions or proposed grammar changes, file an issue
           first; a recorded design decision is a
           prerequisite for any breaking change. **Security
           reports:** through GitHub's private vulnerability
           reporting at
           github.com/cx-home/cx/security/advisories/new — do not
           file a public issue for security-sensitive bugs."""]]]]
